```python
def total(L):
    return 0 if not L else L[0] + total(L[1:])
```

The base case is the empty list, which returns 0. The recursive step is the first item plus the total of the rest. Each call has its own L. L[1:] builds a new list on every call, so the cost grows with the square of the length. Python stops a runaway recursion with RecursionError, at depth 1000 by default. A halving recursion passes n // 2. A global or default-argument object is shared by all calls.

